A||||4||Adrift||How to get this bolt off ?(pic inside)||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-22-2001||08:45 PM||||I need to replace my axle, and according to my Haynes I need to take this bolt out (the bolt I'm refering too is in the centre of the area where the wheel hub goes). The bolt seems huge though, and I don't have any sockets that will fit over it for me to rachet it out... any body know the size of it?<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP_1673.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||jelly2m8||12-22-2001||09:10 PM||||You mean the axle nut?<BR>Its 30mm, you need a deep socket for it. I think a 1-1/8 deep socket will work also.<P>I use an impact gun, but you should be able to make do with a long breaker bar, and by supporting the hub from spining.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Adrift||12-22-2001||09:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>supporting the hub from spining.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What's the best way to do that? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||jelly2m8||12-22-2001||10:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B> What's the best way to do that? <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Seeing that the brakes are allready off, try a pry bar, or crow bar, or something similar.<P> stick one end between 2 of the wheel studs, and let the other end rest on the floor at a 45* angle. Hold the bar so that it doesn't slip off the studs on you, and it will keep the hub from turning so you can get the nut off.<P>good luck<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||12-22-2001||10:11 PM||||30mm deep well socket.<BR>To keep it from spinning,<BR>Just use a socket to hold it thru the cut-out that goes to the back bolts.||||reg||1
